# A helper that gets the current working directory of the shell in the latest
# terminal window.
#
# For terminal emulators running in server-client mode (e.g., foot, kitty, etc),
# the `pid` field found in the `swaymsg -t get_tree` output for the focused
# container is the PID of the terminal server process rather then the terminal
# client process. The interactive shell running in each terminal window is a
# child process spawned by the terminal server process. In other words, it is
# the server process that draws the terminal window, spawns the shell, and
# handles the I/O between the shell via pty devices. The role of a client is
# merely to accept user instructions and request the server to create a new
# terminal window.
#
# The process tree for foot running in server-client mode with two terminal
# windows:
#
#     sway-+-foot-+-sh (A)
#          |      `-sh (B)
#          |-footclient (A)
#          `-footclient (B)
#
# The process tree for kitty running in single-instance mode with two terminal
# windows:
#
#     sway-+-kitty-+-kitten (A)
#                  |-kitten (B)
#                  |-sh (A)
#                  `-sh (B)
#
# Unfortunately, there is no easy way to tell which shell is associated with
# which terminal window. It could be achieved using some tricks like maintaining
# a map between the Sway container id for each terminal client surface and the
# PID of the associated shell, but this brings performance overhead and extra
# maintenance.
#
# As a trade-off, this script simply takes the PID of the latest shell spawned
# by the terminal server instead of strictly finding the PID of the shell
# associated with the focused terminal window. Therefore, this script won't work
# if you expect to open a new terminal with the same working directory as in any
# focused terminal window.
#
# Requires:   jq, sway

# Get the PID of currently focused container.
# NOTE: For terminal running in server-client mode, you always get the PID of
# the server process rather then the PID of the client process, because it is
# actually server process that manages the terminal windows via Sway protocols.
ppid="$(swaymsg -t get_tree | jq -r '.. | select(.type? == "con" and .focused? == true).pid')"
[ -z "${ppid}" ] && printf '%s' "${HOME}"

# Get the PID of the latest spawned shell by terminal
pid="$(pgrep --newest --parent "${ppid}")";
[ -z "${pid}" ] && printf '%s' "${HOME}"

if path="$(readlink -e "/proc/${pid}/cwd")" && [ -n "${path}" ]; then
  printf '%s' "${path}"
else
  printf '%s' "${HOME}"
fi
